+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How the two quantization paths compare:</strong> 1) <strong>Dynamic quantization:</strong> <code>quantize_dynamic(model_fp32, {torch.nn.Linear}, dtype=torch.qint8)</code> rewrites every <code>nn.Linear</code> to INT8 on-the-fly — no calibration data required, but only Linear layers are converted. 2) The <code>torch.save</code> + <code>os.path.getsize</code> block measures the impact: ~418 MB FP32 vs ~106 MB INT8, a 3.9× reduction. 3) <strong>Static ONNX quantization:</strong> <code>ORTQuantizer.from_pretrained(model_path)</code> + <code>AutoQuantizationConfig.avx512(is_static=False, per_channel=False)</code> + <code>quantizer.quantize(save_dir="./bert-quantized", quantization_config=qconfig)</code> exports the model through ONNX Runtime with hardware-tuned INT8 kernels for production x86 CPUs (use <code>avx512()</code> on modern Intel/AMD, <code>arm64()</code> on ARM servers).</p>'

