/* ── LoRA formula ────────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h4 style="color:#c8a96e;margin:24px 0 10px">2.1 — LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation</h4>'
+ '<p class="l-text">LoRA (Hu et al., 2021) is the dominant PEFT technique. The key insight: weight updates during fine-tuning have low <em>intrinsic dimensionality</em>. Instead of updating W directly (a d×d matrix), inject two small matrices A (r×d) and B (d×r) where r &lt;&lt; d. Only A and B are trained.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-formula">'
+ '<div class="cf-title">LoRA: Low-Rank Weight Decomposition</div>'
+ '<div class="cf-math">W\' = W + B·A</div>'
+ '<div class="cf-sub">W ∈ ℝ<sup>d×d</sup> (frozen) &nbsp;|&nbsp; B ∈ ℝ<sup>d×r</sup> (trained) &nbsp;|&nbsp; A ∈ ℝ<sup>r×d</sup> (trained) &nbsp;|&nbsp; r ≪ d</div>'
+ '<div class="cf-sub" style="margin-top:8px">Example: d=768, r=8 → W has 589,824 params, B+A has 12,288 params (2% of W)</div>'
+ '<div class="cf-sub" style="margin-top:4px">Trainable params = 2 × r × d per LoRA layer &nbsp;·&nbsp; Typical r values: 4, 8, 16</div>'
+ '</div>'

