/* ── PEFT code ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h4 style="color:#c8a96e;margin:24px 0 10px">2.2 — PEFT Library</h4>'
+ '<p class="l-text">HuggingFace\'s <code>peft</code> library applies LoRA in three lines of configuration. It automatically identifies which layers to inject LoRA into (typically the attention Q and V projections) and wraps the model.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> LoRA with PEFT Library <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ '<span class="cm"># pip install peft</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> AutoModelForSequenceClassification, AutoTokenizer\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> peft <span class="kw">import</span> LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Load base model (frozen by default in PEFT)</span>\n'
+ 'model = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.<span class="fn">from_pretrained</span>(\n'
+ '    <span class="str">"bert-base-uncased"</span>, num_labels=<span class="num">2</span>\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Configure LoRA</span>\n'
+ 'lora_config = <span class="fn">LoraConfig</span>(\n'
+ '    task_type=TaskType.SEQ_CLS,      <span class="cm"># sequence classification</span>\n'
+ '    r=<span class="num">8</span>,                              <span class="cm"># rank of low-rank matrices</span>\n'
+ '    lora_alpha=<span class="num">32</span>,                    <span class="cm"># scaling factor (alpha/r = effective lr scaling)</span>\n'
+ '    lora_dropout=<span class="num">0.1</span>,                 <span class="cm"># dropout on LoRA layers</span>\n'
+ '    target_modules=[<span class="str">"query"</span>, <span class="str">"value"</span>] <span class="cm"># inject into Q and V projections</span>\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Wrap model with LoRA</span>\n'
+ 'peft_model = <span class="fn">get_peft_model</span>(model, lora_config)\n'
+ 'peft_model.<span class="fn">print_trainable_parameters</span>()\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Output: trainable params: 592,130 || all params: 109,886,980 || trainable%: 0.54</span>\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Training is identical to standard fine-tuning</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> TrainingArguments, Trainer\n\n'
+ 'training_args = <span class="fn">TrainingArguments</span>(\n'
+ '    output_dir=<span class="str">"./lora-sentiment"</span>,\n'
+ '    num_train_epochs=<span class="num">3</span>,\n'
+ '    per_device_train_batch_size=<span class="num">32</span>,   <span class="cm"># can use larger batches — less memory!</span>\n'
+ '    learning_rate=<span class="num">3e-4</span>,               <span class="cm"># LoRA benefits from higher LR than full FT</span>\n'
+ '    warmup_ratio=<span class="num">0.1</span>,\n'
+ '    eval_strategy=<span class="str">"epoch"</span>,           <span class="cm"># transformers 4.46+</span>\n'
+ '    save_strategy=<span class="str">"epoch"</span>,\n'
+ '    load_best_model_at_end=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n'
+ '    fp16=<span class="kw">True</span>,\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ 'trainer = <span class="fn">Trainer</span>(\n'
+ '    model=peft_model,\n'
+ '    args=training_args,\n'
+ '    train_dataset=train_dataset,\n'
+ '    eval_dataset=eval_dataset,\n'
+ '    compute_metrics=compute_metrics,\n'
+ ')\n'
+ 'trainer.<span class="fn">train</span>()\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Save and reload LoRA adapter only (~2 MB vs 418 MB for full model)</span>\n'
+ 'peft_model.<span class="fn">save_pretrained</span>(<span class="str">"./lora-adapter"</span>)\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Reload:</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># from peft import PeftModel</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># base = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained("bert-base-uncased")</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># model = PeftModel.from_pretrained(base, "./lora-adapter")</span>'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How LoRA fine-tuning is wired up:</strong> 1) <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained("bert-base-uncased", num_labels=2)</code> loads the base BERT with a classification head; PEFT freezes the base weights automatically. 2) <code>LoraConfig(task_type=TaskType.SEQ_CLS, r=8, lora_alpha=32, lora_dropout=0.1, target_modules=["query", "value"])</code> declares the LoRA rank, the alpha scaling and which attention projections receive adapter matrices. 3) <code>peft_model = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)</code> + <code>peft_model.print_trainable_parameters()</code> wraps the model and confirms only ~592 K of the ~109 M parameters are trainable — 0.54%. 4) The Trainer block reuses the standard <code>Trainer(model=peft_model, ...)</code> pattern but with <code>learning_rate=3e-4</code> (LoRA tolerates higher LRs) and larger batches; <code>peft_model.save_pretrained("./lora-adapter")</code> saves just the ~2 MB adapter weights.</p>'

/* ── calc-compare: full FT vs LoRA ──────────────────────── */
+ '<div class="calc-compare">'
+ '<div class="compare-col">'
+ '<div class="compare-title" style="color:#ff4b4b">Full Fine-Tuning</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Trainable params</span><span class="ci-val">110M (BERT-base)</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">GPU memory (BS=16)</span><span class="ci-val">~14 GB</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Training time</span><span class="ci-val">~45 min/epoch</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Saved checkpoint</span><span class="ci-val">418 MB</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">F1 on IMDB</span><span class="ci-val">93.1%</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">LR used</span><span class="ci-val">2e-5</span></div>'
+ '</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-col">'
+ '<div class="compare-title" style="color:#4ecdc4">LoRA (r=8)</div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Trainable params</span><span class="ci-val">592K (0.54%)</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">GPU memory (BS=16)</span><span class="ci-val">~6 GB</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Training time</span><span class="ci-val">~18 min/epoch</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">Saved adapter</span><span class="ci-val">~2 MB</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">F1 on IMDB</span><span class="ci-val">92.7%</span></div>'
+ '<div class="compare-item"><span class="ci-label">LR used</span><span class="ci-val">3e-4</span></div>'
+ '</div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="l-note">QLoRA (Dettmers et al., 2023) extends LoRA by loading the <em>base model</em> in 4-bit NF4 quantization, then applying full-precision LoRA adapters on top. This allows fine-tuning a 65B-parameter LLaMA model on a single 48 GB GPU. For production fine-tuning, QLoRA is the recommended approach when you work with models larger than BERT-large on commodity hardware.</div>'

/* ── 3. FastAPI Serving ──────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Section 3 — API Serving with FastAPI</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">FastAPI is the standard choice for ML inference APIs: async-native, type-safe via Pydantic, auto-generates OpenAPI docs, and handles concurrent requests well. The pattern is: load model once at startup, expose a prediction endpoint, validate inputs/outputs with Pydantic schemas.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> FastAPI Inference Server <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ '<span class="cm"># api.py — FastAPI inference server</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> fastapi <span class="kw">import</span> FastAPI, HTTPException\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> pydantic <span class="kw">import</span> BaseModel, Field\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> contextlib <span class="kw">import</span> asynccontextmanager\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> pipeline\n'
+ '<span class="kw">import</span> uvicorn\n'
+ '<span class="kw">import</span> time\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># ── Request / Response Schemas ──────────────────────────</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">PredictRequest</span>(BaseModel):\n'
+ '    text: <span class="fn">str</span> = <span class="fn">Field</span>(..., min_length=<span class="num">1</span>, max_length=<span class="num">512</span>,\n'
+ '                      description=<span class="str">"Text to classify"</span>)\n'
+ '    return_scores: <span class="fn">bool</span> = <span class="fn">Field</span>(<span class="kw">False</span>, description=<span class="str">"Return all class scores"</span>)\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">PredictResponse</span>(BaseModel):\n'
+ '    label: <span class="fn">str</span>\n'
+ '    confidence: <span class="fn">float</span>\n'
+ '    scores: <span class="fn">dict</span> | <span class="kw">None</span> = <span class="kw">None</span>\n'
+ '    latency_ms: <span class="fn">float</span>\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">BatchRequest</span>(BaseModel):\n'
+ '    texts: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">str</span>] = <span class="fn">Field</span>(..., min_length=<span class="num">1</span>, max_items=<span class="num">64</span>)\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># ── Lifespan: load model once at startup ────────────────</span>\n'
+ 'classifier = <span class="kw">None</span>\n\n'
+ '@asynccontextmanager\n'
+ '<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">lifespan</span>(app: FastAPI):\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">global</span> classifier\n'
+ '    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Loading model..."</span>)\n'
+ '    classifier = <span class="fn">pipeline</span>(\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"text-classification"</span>,\n'
+ '        model=<span class="str">"./sentiment-bert-finetuned"</span>,\n'
+ '        device=-<span class="num">1</span>,           <span class="cm"># -1=CPU, 0=first GPU</span>\n'
+ '        top_k=<span class="kw">None</span>  <span class="cm"># modern API; return_all_scores=True deprecated</span>\n'
+ '    )\n'
+ '    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="str">"Model ready."</span>)\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">yield</span>\n'
+ '    classifier = <span class="kw">None</span>  <span class="cm"># cleanup on shutdown</span>\n\n'
+ 'app = <span class="fn">FastAPI</span>(\n'
+ '    title=<span class="str">"Sentiment Analysis API"</span>,\n'
+ '    version=<span class="str">"1.0.0"</span>,\n'
+ '    lifespan=lifespan\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># ── Single prediction endpoint ──────────────────────────</span>\n'
+ '@app.<span class="fn">post</span>(<span class="str">"/predict"</span>, response_model=PredictResponse)\n'
+ '<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict</span>(req: PredictRequest):\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">if</span> classifier <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:\n'
+ '        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">HTTPException</span>(<span class="num">503</span>, <span class="str">"Model not loaded"</span>)\n'
+ '    t0 = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n'
+ '    results = <span class="fn">classifier</span>(req.text)[<span class="num">0</span>]  <span class="cm"># list of {label, score}</span>\n'
+ '    latency = (time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - t0) * <span class="num">1000</span>\n'
+ '    best = <span class="fn">max</span>(results, key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x[<span class="str">"score"</span>])\n'
+ '    scores = {r[<span class="str">"label"</span>]: <span class="fn">round</span>(r[<span class="str">"score"</span>], <span class="num">4</span>) <span class="kw">for</span> r <span class="kw">in</span> results}\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">PredictResponse</span>(\n'
+ '        label=best[<span class="str">"label"</span>],\n'
+ '        confidence=<span class="fn">round</span>(best[<span class="str">"score"</span>], <span class="num">4</span>),\n'
+ '        scores=scores <span class="kw">if</span> req.return_scores <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="kw">None</span>,\n'
+ '        latency_ms=<span class="fn">round</span>(latency, <span class="num">2</span>)\n'
+ '    )\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># ── Batch endpoint for throughput ──────────────────────</span>\n'
+ '@app.<span class="fn">post</span>(<span class="str">"/predict/batch"</span>)\n'
+ '<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict_batch</span>(req: BatchRequest):\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">if</span> classifier <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">None</span>:\n'
+ '        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">HTTPException</span>(<span class="num">503</span>, <span class="str">"Model not loaded"</span>)\n'
+ '    t0 = time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>()\n'
+ '    <span class="cm"># batch inference is significantly faster than N single calls</span>\n'
+ '    all_results = <span class="fn">classifier</span>(req.texts, batch_size=<span class="num">16</span>)\n'
+ '    latency = (time.<span class="fn">perf_counter</span>() - t0) * <span class="num">1000</span>\n'
+ '    predictions = []\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">for</span> res <span class="kw">in</span> all_results:\n'
+ '        best = <span class="fn">max</span>(res, key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x[<span class="str">"score"</span>])\n'
+ '        predictions.<span class="fn">append</span>({<span class="str">"label"</span>: best[<span class="str">"label"</span>],\n'
+ '                             <span class="str">"confidence"</span>: <span class="fn">round</span>(best[<span class="str">"score"</span>], <span class="num">4</span>)})\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">return</span> {\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"predictions"</span>: predictions,\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"count"</span>: <span class="fn">len</span>(predictions),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"total_latency_ms"</span>: <span class="fn">round</span>(latency, <span class="num">2</span>),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"avg_latency_ms"</span>: <span class="fn">round</span>(latency / <span class="fn">len</span>(req.texts), <span class="num">2</span>)\n'
+ '    }\n\n'
+ '@app.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"/health"</span>)\n'
+ '<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">health</span>(): <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="str">"status"</span>: <span class="str">"ok"</span>, <span class="str">"model_loaded"</span>: classifier <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="kw">None</span>}\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">if</span> __name__ == <span class="str">"__main__"</span>:\n'
+ '    uvicorn.<span class="fn">run</span>(app, host=<span class="str">"0.0.0.0"</span>, port=<span class="num">8000</span>, workers=<span class="num">2</span>)\n'
+ '    <span class="cm"># Access docs at: http://localhost:8000/docs</span>'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How this FastAPI service serves the model:</strong> 1) <strong>Lifespan + global classifier:</strong> the <code>@asynccontextmanager</code> + <code>lifespan(app)</code> pattern loads <code>pipeline("text-classification", model="./sentiment-bert-finetuned", device=-1, top_k=None)</code> exactly once at startup and tears it down on shutdown — no per-request reload. 2) <strong>Single endpoint:</strong> <code>@app.post("/predict", response_model=PredictResponse)</code> validates input with Pydantic, runs <code>classifier(req.text)</code>, picks the top label via <code>max(results, key=lambda x: x["score"])</code> and returns label, confidence, optional all-class scores, and measured <code>latency_ms</code>. 3) <strong>Batch endpoint:</strong> <code>@app.post("/predict/batch")</code> uses <code>classifier(req.texts, batch_size=16)</code> for true batched inference — far higher throughput than N single calls. 4) <strong>Health + entry point:</strong> <code>/health</code> reports model status, and <code>uvicorn.run(app, host="0.0.0.0", port=8000, workers=2)</code> launches the production server.</p>'

/* ── 4. Gradio Demo ──────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Section 4 — Gradio Interactive Demo</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Gradio lets you build a shareable web demo for your model in minutes. It is the standard way to demonstrate NLP models to non-technical stakeholders, product managers, and reviewers. One URL, no setup required on their side.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON (DEMO)</span> Gradio Demo Interface <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ '<span class="cm"># demo.py — Gradio interface in ~15 lines</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">import</span> gradio <span class="kw">as</span> gr\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> transformers <span class="kw">import</span> pipeline\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Load model once</span>\n'
+ 'classifier = <span class="fn">pipeline</span>(\n'
+ '    <span class="str">"text-classification"</span>,\n'
+ '    model=<span class="str">"./sentiment-bert-finetuned"</span>,\n'
+ '    top_k=<span class="kw">None</span>  <span class="cm"># modern API; return_all_scores=True deprecated</span>\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">analyze_sentiment</span>(text: <span class="fn">str</span>) -> <span class="fn">dict</span>:\n'
+ '    <span class="str">"""Return label → confidence mapping for Gradio Label component."""</span>\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> text.<span class="fn">strip</span>():\n'
+ '        <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="str">"Please enter some text."</span>: <span class="num">1.0</span>}\n'
+ '    results = <span class="fn">classifier</span>(text[:<span class="num">512</span>])[<span class="num">0</span>]\n'
+ '    <span class="cm"># Gradio Label expects {label: score} dict</span>\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">return</span> {r[<span class="str">"label"</span>]: r[<span class="str">"score"</span>] <span class="kw">for</span> r <span class="kw">in</span> results}\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Build the interface</span>\n'
+ 'demo = gr.<span class="fn">Interface</span>(\n'
+ '    fn=analyze_sentiment,\n'
+ '    inputs=gr.<span class="fn">Textbox</span>(\n'
+ '        label=<span class="str">"Input Text"</span>,\n'
+ '        placeholder=<span class="str">"Enter a sentence to analyze..."</span>,\n'
+ '        lines=<span class="num">4</span>\n'
+ '    ),\n'
+ '    outputs=gr.<span class="fn">Label</span>(\n'
+ '        label=<span class="str">"Sentiment"</span>,\n'
+ '        num_top_classes=<span class="num">2</span>\n'
+ '    ),\n'
+ '    title=<span class="str">"BERT Sentiment Analyzer"</span>,\n'
+ '    description=<span class="str">"Fine-tuned on IMDB movie reviews. Achieves 93.1% accuracy."</span>,\n'
+ '    examples=[\n'
+ '        [<span class="str">"This movie was absolutely brilliant and deeply moving."</span>],\n'
+ '        [<span class="str">"A complete waste of time. Terrible acting and no plot."</span>],\n'
+ '        [<span class="str">"It was okay. Some good moments, some slow parts."</span>],\n'
+ '    ],\n'
+ '    theme=gr.themes.<span class="fn">Soft</span>()\n'
+ ')\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Launch locally</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">if</span> __name__ == <span class="str">"__main__"</span>:\n'
+ '    demo.<span class="fn">launch</span>(server_port=<span class="num">7860</span>, share=<span class="kw">False</span>)\n'
+ '    <span class="cm"># share=True creates a public ngrok URL (valid 72 hours)</span>'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How this 15-line Gradio app comes together:</strong> 1) <code>pipeline("text-classification", model="./sentiment-bert-finetuned", top_k=None)</code> loads the fine-tuned classifier once at module-import time. 2) <code>analyze_sentiment(text)</code> returns a <code>{label: score, ...}</code> dict — exactly the shape that <code>gr.Label(num_top_classes=2)</code> expects for its built-in bar chart. 3) <code>gr.Interface(fn=analyze_sentiment, inputs=gr.Textbox(...), outputs=gr.Label(...), title=..., description=..., examples=[...], theme=gr.themes.Soft())</code> declaratively builds the page with example prompts and a polished theme. 4) <code>demo.launch(server_port=7860, share=False)</code> runs locally; setting <code>share=True</code> instead creates a temporary public ngrok URL for sharing the demo with anyone.</p>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>Deploying to HuggingFace Spaces:</strong> Spaces is a free hosting platform for Gradio (and Streamlit) demos. Push your <code>demo.py</code> and <code>requirements.txt</code> to a new Space repository, and HuggingFace builds and hosts it automatically. Your demo gets a permanent URL at <code>huggingface.co/spaces/&lt;username&gt;/&lt;space-name&gt;</code>. CPU-tier Spaces are free; GPU-tier costs a few dollars per hour. For a portfolio or recruiter-facing demo, CPU is sufficient for BERT inference.</div>'

/* ── 5. Docker Deployment ────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Section 5 — Docker Packaging</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Docker containers are the standard unit of deployment in production. They package your model, dependencies, and runtime into a single image that runs identically on any machine — your laptop, a VPS, a Kubernetes cluster, or AWS ECS.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>DOCKER</span> Production Docker Image <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ '<span class="cm"># Dockerfile — multi-stage build for lean production image</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">FROM</span> python:<span class="num">3.11</span>-slim <span class="kw">AS</span> base\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">WORKDIR</span> /app\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Install system dependencies</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">RUN</span> apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \\\n'
+ '    build-essential curl && \\\n'
+ '    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Copy and install Python requirements first (layer cache optimization)</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">COPY</span> requirements.txt .\n'
+ '<span class="kw">RUN</span> pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Copy model and application code</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">COPY</span> ./sentiment-bert-finetuned ./sentiment-bert-finetuned\n'
+ '<span class="kw">COPY</span> api.py .\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Non-root user for security</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">RUN</span> useradd -m appuser && chown -R appuser:appuser /app\n'
+ '<span class="kw">USER</span> appuser\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">EXPOSE</span> <span class="num">8000</span>\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">HEALTHCHECK</span> --interval=<span class="num">30</span>s --timeout=<span class="num">10</span>s --retries=<span class="num">3</span> \\\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">CMD</span> curl -f http://localhost:<span class="num">8000</span>/health || exit <span class="num">1</span>\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">CMD</span> [<span class="str">"uvicorn"</span>, <span class="str">"api:app"</span>, <span class="str">"--host"</span>, <span class="str">"0.0.0.0"</span>, <span class="str">"--port"</span>, <span class="str">"8000"</span>, <span class="str">"--workers"</span>, <span class="str">"2"</span>]\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># Build and run:</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># docker build -t sentiment-api:v1 .</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># docker run -p 8000:8000 sentiment-api:v1</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm">#</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># requirements.txt:</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># fastapi>=0.104</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># uvicorn[standard]>=0.24</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># transformers>=4.35</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># torch>=2.1 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># pydantic>=2.0</span>\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># onnxruntime>=1.16</span>'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>How this production Dockerfile is structured:</strong> 1) <code>FROM python:3.11-slim AS base</code> + <code>WORKDIR /app</code> + the apt-get layer install minimal build tools and curl for the healthcheck. 2) Copying <code>requirements.txt</code> first and running <code>pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt</code> maximises Docker layer caching — changing application code does not invalidate the dependency layer. 3) Copying the <code>sentiment-bert-finetuned</code> folder and <code>api.py</code>, then creating a non-root <code>appuser</code> + <code>USER appuser</code> follows the container security best practice of avoiding root at runtime. 4) <code>EXPOSE 8000</code> + <code>HEALTHCHECK</code> (curls <code>/health</code> every 30 s) + <code>CMD ["uvicorn", "api:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8000", "--workers", "2"]</code> declares the network interface, liveness probe and the production launch command.</p>'

/* ── 6. Monitoring ───────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Section 6 — Monitoring & Maintenance</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">A deployed model is not a finished product — it is a system that requires ongoing maintenance. Language evolves, user behavior shifts, and model performance degrades silently without monitoring. These are the three pillars of ML system health in production.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card">'
+ '<div class="calc-card-title">Data Drift Detection</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card-body">Monitor the statistical distribution of incoming text: vocabulary shift, average token length, sentiment distribution. A sudden spike in OOV (out-of-vocabulary) tokens signals that users are writing differently than the training corpus. Tools: Evidently AI, Whylogs, custom KL-divergence checks on embedding distributions.</div>'
+ '</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card">'
+ '<div class="calc-card-title">Concept Drift Detection</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card-body">Even when input distributions are stable, the relationship between text and labels can shift. New slang, sarcasm patterns, or domain events (elections, product launches) change what "positive" means in your domain. Monitor prediction confidence histograms — a model becoming less certain is a signal of concept drift.</div>'
+ '</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card">'
+ '<div class="calc-card-title">Latency & Throughput SLOs</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card-body">Define Service Level Objectives before deployment: p50 latency &lt; 50ms, p99 latency &lt; 200ms, error rate &lt; 0.1%, throughput &gt; 100 req/s. Use Prometheus + Grafana for metrics. Alert on p99 exceeding threshold or GPU memory approaching OOM. Never discover problems from user complaints.</div>'
+ '</div>'
+ '</div>'

/* ── Logging code ────────────────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h4 style="color:#c8a96e;margin:24px 0 10px">Logging Predictions</h4>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Log every prediction with its input, output, confidence, and latency. This creates your ground-truth audit trail, enables offline retraining on real distribution, and surfaces systematic errors.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span> Prediction Logger &amp; A/B Testing <button class="code-copy">COPY</button></div><div class="code-block"><pre><code>'
+ '<span class="cm"># prediction_logger.py</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">import</span> json, time, uuid\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> pathlib <span class="kw">import</span> Path\n'
+ '<span class="kw">from</span> datetime <span class="kw">import</span> datetime, timezone\n\n'
+ 'LOG_DIR = <span class="fn">Path</span>(<span class="str">"./prediction_logs"</span>)\n'
+ 'LOG_DIR.<span class="fn">mkdir</span>(exist_ok=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">log_prediction</span>(text: <span class="fn">str</span>, label: <span class="fn">str</span>, confidence: <span class="fn">float</span>,\n'
+ '                   latency_ms: <span class="fn">float</span>, request_id: <span class="fn">str</span> = <span class="kw">None</span>) -> <span class="fn">str</span>:\n'
+ '    <span class="str">"""Append a JSON log line for each prediction."""</span>\n'
+ '    entry = {\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"id"</span>: request_id <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="fn">str</span>(uuid.<span class="fn">uuid4</span>())[:<span class="num">8</span>],\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"ts"</span>: datetime.<span class="fn">now</span>(timezone.utc).<span class="fn">isoformat</span>(),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"text_len"</span>: <span class="fn">len</span>(text),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"text_preview"</span>: text[:<span class="num">80</span>].<span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="str">"\\n"</span>, <span class="str">" "</span>),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"label"</span>: label,\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"confidence"</span>: <span class="fn">round</span>(confidence, <span class="num">4</span>),\n'
+ '        <span class="str">"latency_ms"</span>: <span class="fn">round</span>(latency_ms, <span class="num">2</span>)\n'
+ '    }\n'
+ '    <span class="cm"># Daily log files: prediction_logs/2024-01-15.jsonl</span>\n'
+ '    log_file = LOG_DIR / <span class="str">f"{datetime.now().strftime(\'%Y-%m-%d\')}.jsonl"</span>\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">open</span>(log_file, <span class="str">"a"</span>) <span class="kw">as</span> f:\n'
+ '        f.<span class="fn">write</span>(json.<span class="fn">dumps</span>(entry) + <span class="str">"\\n"</span>)\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">return</span> entry[<span class="str">"id"</span>]\n\n'
+ '<span class="cm"># A/B testing: route 10% of traffic to model_v2</span>\n'
+ '<span class="kw">import</span> random\n\n'
+ '<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">predict_with_ab</span>(text: <span class="fn">str</span>, model_a, model_b, b_fraction=<span class="num">0.1</span>):\n'
+ '    <span class="str">"""Route traffic between two models, log which variant was used."""</span>\n'
+ '    use_b = random.<span class="fn">random</span>() &lt; b_fraction\n'
+ '    model = model_b <span class="kw">if</span> use_b <span class="kw">else</span> model_a\n'
+ '    result = <span class="fn">model</span>(text)[<span class="num">0</span>]\n'
+ '    best = <span class="fn">max</span>(result, key=<span class="kw">lambda</span> x: x[<span class="str">"score"</span>])\n'
+ '    <span class="fn">log_prediction</span>(\n'
+ '        text=text,\n'
+ '        label=best[<span class="str">"label"</span>],\n'
+ '        confidence=best[<span class="str">"score"</span>],\n'
+ '        latency_ms=<span class="num">0</span>,\n'
+ '        request_id=<span class="str">f"{\'B\' if use_b else \'A\'}-{uuid.uuid4().hex[:6]}"</span>\n'
+ '    )\n'
+ '    <span class="kw">return</span> best'
+ '</code></pre></div></div>'
+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>What the prediction logger and A/B router do:</strong> 1) <code>LOG_DIR = Path("./prediction_logs"); LOG_DIR.mkdir(exist_ok=True)</code> creates a daily-log-friendly directory at import time. 2) <code>log_prediction(text, label, confidence, latency_ms, request_id)</code> builds a JSON record (UUID, ISO-8601 UTC timestamp, text length, 80-char preview, label, confidence, latency) and appends one line to <code>YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl</code> — a clean format for downstream pandas analysis. 3) <code>predict_with_ab(text, model_a, model_b, b_fraction=0.1)</code> sends 10% of traffic to <code>model_b</code> via <code>random.random() &lt; b_fraction</code>, tags the request id with <code>"A-"</code> or <code>"B-"</code> and logs both variants. 4) The result is a complete audit trail you can later replay to compute per-variant accuracy and latency once a labeled evaluation set exists.</p>'

+ '<div class="l-note"><strong>A/B testing in production:</strong> Never replace a production model with a new version all at once. Use a canary deployment: route 5–10% of traffic to the new model, compare accuracy on a labeled evaluation set built from logged predictions, and only promote if the new model is statistically significantly better. A small confidence interval requires hundreds of labeled examples — build your annotation pipeline before you need it.</div>'

/* ── 7. Deployment Checklist ─────────────────────────────── */
+ '<h3 class="l-section-title">Section 7 — Complete Deployment Checklist</h3>'
+ '<p class="l-text">Use this checklist as a reference before every production deployment. Each step has caused a real production incident when skipped.</p>'

+ '<div class="calc-steps">'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Training complete &amp; evaluated</strong> — Model achieves target F1 on held-out test set (not validation). Confusion matrix reviewed. Error analysis done on failure cases. No data leakage.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Model optimized</strong> — ONNX export verified (outputs match PyTorch within 1e-5). Quantization applied if CPU deployment. Benchmark latency and throughput measured on target hardware.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-body"><strong>API tested</strong> — Unit tests for all endpoints. Integration test with real payloads. Error handling verified (empty input, too-long input, unicode edge cases). Load tested with k6 or locust.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Container built &amp; scanned</strong> — Docker image builds cleanly. No sensitive credentials in image layers. Security scan run (Trivy or Snyk). Image size reasonable (&lt;2 GB for CPU deployments).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-body"><strong>SLOs defined</strong> — p50/p99 latency targets documented. Throughput requirements verified against expected traffic. Memory usage profiled under load. Autoscaling configured if needed.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">6</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Monitoring configured</strong> — Prometheus metrics scraping active. Grafana dashboard deployed with latency, error rate, and throughput panels. Alerting rules set with PagerDuty or email. Prediction logging enabled with rotation policy.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">7</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Canary deployment</strong> — New model version deployed to 10% of traffic. Metrics compared against v1 baseline for 24–48 hours. Rollback procedure documented and tested. Promotion criteria defined (e.g., p99 &lt; 200ms AND F1 delta &gt; 0).</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-step"><div class="step-num">8</div><div class="step-body"><strong>Full rollout &amp; documentation</strong> — Traffic promoted to 100% after canary validation. API documentation updated (OpenAPI spec). Model card published to HuggingFace Hub with training details, dataset, metrics, and limitations. Retraining schedule established.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

